{"test_id": "B06-001", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "Why does CCoP 2.0 require complex passwords rather than just long passwords?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Character diversity increases search space exponentially", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.2"}, {"fact": "Blocks common dictionary-based attacks", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.2"}, {"fact": "Balances usability with security strength", "tier": "supporting", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.2"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not prescribe password complexity rules in its main control clauses; it defers to industry standards such as NIST SP 800-63B for password/credential requirements (per Response to Feedback). The general security intent of credential controls (in conjunction with §5.1.2 authorisation/authentication and §5.7.2(b) MFA) is to prevent unauthorised access by raising the cost of credential attacks (brute-force, dictionary). Reference: Response to Feedback §11.29; CCoP 2.0 §5.1.2.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 5.2.2", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for password complexity - preventing brute force", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.1.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Response to Feedback §11.29"]}}
{"test_id": "B06-002", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "What is the primary security objective of multi-factor authentication requirements?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Single factor compromise insufficient for access", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.3"}, {"fact": "Protects against phishing and credential theft", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.3"}, {"fact": "Provides layered authentication defense", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.3"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 Section 5.3.1(c) regarding mfa - preventing credential theft impact is: Limit impact of compromised credentials through defense in depth.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Single factor compromise insufficient for access (critical)\n- Protects against phishing and credential theft (critical)\n- Provides layered authentication defense (important)\n\n\nUnderstanding control intent helps in implementing effective security measures that address the underlying threat rather than merely satisfying checkbox requirements. Organizations should consider both the letter and spirit of each control.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §5.3.1", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 5.2.3", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for mfa - preventing credential theft impact", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.1.2", "5.3.1", "5.7.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "clause_reference_original": ["5.1.2", "5.3.1", "5.7.2"], "clause_reference_corrected_by": "agent-team-2026-04-26", "clause_reference_corrected_verdict": "PARTIAL_ACCEPT"}}
{"test_id": "B06-003", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "Why are there different reporting timelines for critical vs other incidents?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Critical incidents may require cross-sector coordination", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 7.3.1"}, {"fact": "2-hour window enables CSA to activate national response", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 7.3.1"}, {"fact": "Tiered reporting aligns resource allocation with severity", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 7.3.1"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of differentiated incident reporting timelines (per Cybersecurity Act §14, with prescribed periods set in the Cybersecurity (Critical Information Infrastructure) Regulations) is to enable timely national response by aligning notification urgency with incident severity.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Faster reporting for prescribed/severe incidents enables CSA to coordinate cross-sector response (critical)\n- Tiered reporting aligns resource allocation with incident severity (critical)\n- Provides a single-point national view of CII-impacting incidents (important)\n\nReference: Cybersecurity Act 2018 §14; CCoP 2.0 §7.1 (Incident Management) for response obligations.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 7.3.1", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for incident reporting timeline - enabling response", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "7 (with statutory basis in Cybersecurity Act §14)", "clause_reference": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §14"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["CCoP 2.0 §7.1.1"]}}
{"test_id": "B06-004", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "What is the security purpose of requiring 12-month log retention?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Supports investigation of long-running compromises", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.3.2"}, {"fact": "Enables trend analysis and pattern detection", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.3.2"}, {"fact": "12 months covers typical dwell time detection gap", "tier": "supporting", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.3.2"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 §6.1.4(c) requiring a minimum 12-month log retention is to enable retrospective forensic investigation and threat hunting.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Supports investigation of long-running compromises (critical)\n- Enables trend analysis and pattern detection (important)\n- 12 months covers typical adversary dwell-time gaps (supporting)\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §6.1.4(c).", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 6.3.2", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for log retention - forensic investigation", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "6", "clause_reference": ["6.1.4"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B06-005", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "How does network segmentation contribute to CII security beyond just access control?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Prevents compromise propagation across systems", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.4.1"}, {"fact": "Enables granular security policy enforcement", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.4.1"}, {"fact": "Reduces blast radius of successful attacks", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.4.1"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 §5.5.1 (with §5.5.2) regarding network segmentation is to contain breaches and limit lateral movement.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Prevents compromise propagation across systems (critical)\n- Reduces blast radius of successful attacks (critical)\n- Enables granular policy enforcement at segment boundaries (important)\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §5.5.1, §5.5.2.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 5.4.1", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for network segmentation - blast radius reduction", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.5.1", "5.5.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B06-006", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "What security outcome does regular vulnerability scanning aim to achieve?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Identifies known exploitable weaknesses before attackers", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.2.1"}, {"fact": "Prioritizes remediation based on risk", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.2.1"}, {"fact": "Maintains continuous security posture visibility", "tier": "supporting", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.2.1"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 §5.14 (Vulnerability Assessment) is to reduce the attackable surface by finding and remediating exploitable vulnerabilities before adversaries do.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Identifies known exploitable weaknesses before attackers (critical)\n- Prioritizes remediation based on risk (important, per §5.14.2)\n- Maintains continuous security posture visibility through periodic assessments (supporting, per §5.14.3)\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §5.14.1, §5.14.2, §5.14.3.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 6.2.1", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for vulnerability scanning - exposure reduction", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.14.1", "5.14.2", "5.14.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B06-007", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "How does penetration testing complement vulnerability scanning for CII security?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Tests exploitation chains not visible to scanners", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.2.3"}, {"fact": "Validates defense-in-depth through attack paths", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.2.3"}, {"fact": "Identifies process and human factor vulnerabilities", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.2.3"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 §5.15 (Penetration Testing) is to validate control effectiveness through adversarial simulation.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Tests exploitation chains not visible to scanners (critical)\n- Validates defence-in-depth through realistic attack paths (critical)\n- Identifies process and human factor vulnerabilities (important)\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §5.15.1, §5.15.2.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 6.2.3", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for penetration testing - validation of controls", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.15.1", "5.15.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B06-008", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "Why does CCoP require periodic restoration testing in addition to regular backups?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Unverified backups may fail to restore", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.6.1"}, {"fact": "Tests RTO/RPO achievement in practice", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.6.1"}, {"fact": "Validates backup integrity and completeness", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.6.1"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 §8.1.4 (testing restoration of backups) - supplementing §8.1.1 (backup and restoration plan) and §8.1.2 (perform backups) - is to ensure actual recovery capability, not merely backup creation.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Unverified backups may fail to restore (critical)\n- Tests recovery objectives in practice (critical)\n- Validates backup integrity and completeness (important)\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §8.1.1, §8.1.4.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 5.6.1", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for backup testing - recovery assurance", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "8", "clause_reference": ["8.1.1", "8.1.4"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B06-009", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "What threat model makes encryption in transit mandatory for all CII communications?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Prevents interception of sensitive data on networks", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.5.1"}, {"fact": "Protects against session hijacking", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.5.1"}, {"fact": "Ensures data confidentiality across infrastructure boundaries", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.5.1"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 §5.7.2(c) (strong encryption for remote connections) and §5.17 (Cryptographic Key Management) is to protect against network eavesdropping and man-in-the-middle attacks where communications traverse untrusted or external networks.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Prevents interception of sensitive data in transit (critical)\n- Protects against session hijacking and tampering (critical)\n- Ensures key material itself is protected (important, per §5.17)\n\nNote: CCoP does not impose a blanket 'encryption for all CII communications' rule; the explicit mandate covers remote connections (§5.7.2). Internal segment-to-segment encryption is risk-based per §3.5.1 defence-in-depth.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §5.7.2(c), §5.17.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 5.5.1", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for encryption - data protection in transit", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.7.2", "5.17"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B06-010", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "What is the primary security outcome of mandatory security awareness training?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Addresses human element as common attack vector", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 8.2.1"}, {"fact": "Creates security-conscious culture", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 8.2.1"}, {"fact": "Reduces susceptibility to phishing and social engineering", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 8.2.1"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 §9.1.1 (cybersecurity awareness programme) and §9.2.1 (cybersecurity skills training for personnel operating/managing CII) is to address the human element as a major attack vector.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Reduces susceptibility to phishing and social engineering (critical)\n- Addresses human element as common attack vector (critical)\n- Builds a security-conscious culture across users, operators and external vendors (important)\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §9.1.1, §9.2.1.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 8.2.1", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for security training - human firewall", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "9", "clause_reference": ["9.1.1", "9.2.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B06-011", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "What supply chain risks does CCoP vendor due diligence aim to address?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Prevents compromise via trusted vendor relationships", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 9.1.2"}, {"fact": "Addresses vendor's security posture as attack surface", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 9.1.2"}, {"fact": "Ensures continuity if vendor experiences incident", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 9.1.2"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain an explicit vendor due-diligence clause. Where it touches third-party risk, it does so by extending CIIO obligations to cover external vendors and their personnel - e.g., §9.1.1 (awareness programme covers external vendors), §7.1.5 (IRP communicated to external vendors), §5.15.3-§5.15.4 (third-party penetration testers must be CREST-accredited and supervised). The general security intent is to manage risk introduced via trusted third-party relationships by ensuring vendors operate to the same controls as the CIIO.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §9.1.1, §7.1.5, §5.15.3-§5.15.4 (no dedicated vendor due-diligence clause exists in the corpus).", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 9.1.2", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for vendor due diligence - supply chain risk", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "9", "clause_reference": ["9.1.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["CCoP 2.0 §9.1.1", "§7.1.5; CCoP 2.0 corpus does not impose explicit vendor due diligence"]}}
{"test_id": "B06-012", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "How does change management contribute to CII security beyond operational stability?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Changes can inadvertently weaken security controls", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.4.1"}, {"fact": "Provides audit trail of configuration modifications", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.4.1"}, {"fact": "Enables rollback of security-impacting changes", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.4.1"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not have a stand-alone change-management clause; instead it embeds change-related security checks in §5.14.4 (re-perform vulnerability assessment after major system changes) and §5.15.2 (re-perform penetration testing after major system changes). The security intent is to prevent security degradation from uncontrolled or untested changes by requiring re-assessment of CII after major modifications (commissioning new systems, new application modules, system upgrades, technology refresh).\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Mandatory vulnerability/pen-test after major changes (critical)\n- Catches security regressions introduced by changes (critical)\n- Provides evidence of post-change security posture (important)\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §5.14.4, §5.15.2.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 6.4.1", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for change management - stability and security", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.14.4", "5.15.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B06-013", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "What security problem does periodic access review specifically address?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Users accumulate access over time without losing old rights", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.5"}, {"fact": "Excessive access increases breach impact", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.5"}, {"fact": "Enforces least privilege over time", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.5"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 Section 5.2.2 regarding access review - privilege creep prevention is: Prevent privilege accumulation beyond actual need.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Users accumulate access over time without losing old rights (critical)\n- Excessive access increases breach impact (critical)\n- Enforces least privilege over time (important)\n\n\nUnderstanding control intent helps in implementing effective security measures that address the underlying threat rather than merely satisfying checkbox requirements. Organizations should consider both the letter and spirit of each control.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 \u00a75.2.1", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 5.2.5", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for access review - privilege creep prevention", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.2.1", "5.2.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "clause_reference_original": ["5.2.1", "5.2.2"], "clause_reference_corrected_by": "agent-team-2026-04-26", "clause_reference_corrected_verdict": "CONFIRMED_WITH_MINOR_DEFECT"}}
{"test_id": "B06-014", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "What is the primary objective of CCoP security monitoring requirements?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Reduces dwell time of undetected compromises", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.3.1"}, {"fact": "Provides visibility into security-relevant events", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.3.1"}, {"fact": "Enables evidence collection for investigation", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.3.1"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 §6.2.1 (Monitoring and Detection) is to enable timely detection of and response to security incidents.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Reduces dwell time of undetected compromises (critical)\n- Provides visibility into security-relevant events through continuous monitoring (critical)\n- Enables evidence collection for investigation (important)\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §6.2.1.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 6.3.1", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for security monitoring - threat detection", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "6", "clause_reference": ["6.2.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B06-015", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "Why does CCoP emphasize data classification as foundation for data protection?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Not all data requires equal protection", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.5.2"}, {"fact": "Classification drives control selection and resource allocation", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.5.2"}, {"fact": "Prevents over-protection of low-value data and under-protection of critical data", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.5.2"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain a dedicated data-classification clause; data sensitivity is addressed indirectly through risk-based principles (e.g. §3.5.1 defence-in-depth, §3.2 risk management) and through the Security By Design Framework adopted under §3.4.1. The security intent of classifying data is to enable protection proportionate to data sensitivity and impact.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Not all data requires equal protection (critical)\n- Classification drives control selection and resource allocation (critical)\n- Prevents over-protection of low-value data and under-protection of critical data (important)\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §3.4.1 (adoption of Security By Design Framework); Security By Design Framework (data classification guidance).", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 5.5.2", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for data classification - protection proportional to sensitivity", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.5.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Security By Design Framework; Risk Assessment Guide for CII"]}}
{"test_id": "B06-016", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "What cybersecurity threats does physical security control address?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Prevents unauthorized device insertion", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.7.1"}, {"fact": "Protects against tampering and theft of equipment", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.7.1"}, {"fact": "Limits insider physical access threats", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.7.1"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain a dedicated physical-security clause in its main body. Physical security is addressed via the Security By Design Framework (adopted under §3.4.1) which covers physical access controls, tamper protection, and environmental controls for CII. The security intent of physical security in a cybersecurity context is to prevent direct physical compromise of systems and data.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Prevents unauthorised device insertion (e.g., USB, rogue endpoints) (critical)\n- Protects against tampering and theft of equipment (critical)\n- Limits insider physical access threats (important)\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §3.4.1; Security By Design Framework (physical security domain).", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 5.7.1", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for physical security - preventing unauthorized physical access", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.7.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Security By Design Framework; CCoP 2.0 §3.4.1"]}}
{"test_id": "B06-017", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "What failure mode does a documented incident response plan address?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Ad-hoc response leads to errors and delays", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 7.2.1"}, {"fact": "Pre-defined roles reduce decision paralysis", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 7.2.1"}, {"fact": "Enables coordination across teams and stakeholders", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 7.2.1"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 §7.1.1 (Cybersecurity Incident Response Plan) is to prevent chaotic and ineffective response to security incidents by mandating a documented plan with CIRT structure, defined roles, escalation procedures and communication paths.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Ad-hoc response leads to errors and delays (critical)\n- Pre-defined roles reduce decision paralysis (important)\n- Enables coordination across teams and stakeholders (critical)\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §7.1.1.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 7.2.1", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for incident response plan - organized response", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "7", "clause_reference": ["7.1.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B06-018", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "How does business continuity planning complement cybersecurity controls?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Controls may fail despite best efforts", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 8.2.1"}, {"fact": "BCP provides recovery path when prevention fails", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 8.2.1"}, {"fact": "Addresses availability impact of incidents", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 8.2.2"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 Section 8.2 regarding business continuity - resilience is: Ensure essential functions despite successful cyber attacks.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Controls may fail despite best efforts (critical)\n- BCP provides recovery path when prevention fails (critical)\n- Addresses availability impact of incidents (important)\n\n\nUnderstanding control intent helps in implementing effective security measures that address the underlying threat rather than merely satisfying checkbox requirements. Organizations should consider both the letter and spirit of each control.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §8.2", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 7.4.1", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for business continuity - resilience", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "8", "clause_reference": ["8.2.1", "8.2.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B06-019", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "What is the security purpose of requiring formal risk assessment for CII operators?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Resources are finite and must be allocated efficiently", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 3.2.1"}, {"fact": "Risk assessment identifies highest-priority vulnerabilities", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 3.2.1"}, {"fact": "Provides basis for control selection and justification", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 3.2.1"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 Section 3.2 regarding risk assessment - informed decision making is: Enable security investment decisions based on measured risk.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Resources are finite and must be allocated efficiently (critical)\n- Risk assessment identifies highest-priority vulnerabilities (critical)\n- Provides basis for control selection and justification (important)\n\n\nUnderstanding control intent helps in implementing effective security measures that address the underlying threat rather than merely satisfying checkbox requirements. Organizations should consider both the letter and spirit of each control.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §3.2", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 4.2.1", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for risk assessment - informed decision making", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "3", "clause_reference": ["3.2.1", "3.2.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Risk Assessment Guide §3"]}}
{"test_id": "B06-020", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "Why does CCoP assign specific accountability to senior management for cybersecurity?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Cybersecurity requires cross-organizational coordination", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 8.1.1"}, {"fact": "Management authority enables resource allocation", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 8.1.1"}, {"fact": "Creates organizational culture of security accountability", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 8.1.1"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not have a single explicit 'senior management accountability' clause. Accountability flows from the Cybersecurity Act 2018 (which places the duty to comply with CCoP and to report incidents on the CIIO as legal owner) and is reinforced internally by §3.2.1(a) which requires the CIIO to promote a 'risk culture' that enables open communication about risk and embraces learning. The security intent is to ensure cybersecurity has organisational authority and resource commitment.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Cybersecurity requires cross-organisational coordination, which only senior leaders can authorise (critical)\n- Management authority enables resource allocation across business units (critical)\n- Creates organisational culture of security accountability (important, per §3.2.1(a))\n\nReference: Cybersecurity Act 2018 §14; CCoP 2.0 §3.2.1(a).", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 8.1.1", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for senior management accountability - organizational commitment", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "3", "clause_reference": ["3.2.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §14"]}}
